problem:
Problem description
- A single-period blending and procurement planning problem is given with a set of crude inputs $$\mathcal{R}$$ and a set of gasoline outputs $$\mathcal{G}$$.
- One distinguished crude type $$r^\star \in \mathcal{R}$$ must appear in each gasoline product at no less than a product-specific minimum share $$\alpha_g$$ for every $$g \in \mathcal{G}$$.
- Each finished gasoline type $$g \in \mathcal{G}$$ has a unit selling price $$s_g$$.
- Each crude type $$r \in \mathcal{R}$$ has an initial inventory level $$I_r$$.
- Some crude types may be purchasable from the market, with a total purchase cap $$\bar P_r$$ for each purchasable crude type $$r$$.
- Market purchases, when available, may be divided into ordered price blocks indexed by $$k \in \mathcal{K}_r$$, each with a block capacity $$U_{r,k}$$ and a unit purchase cost $$c_{r,k}$$.
- The planning decision determines how much of each crude type to purchase from each price block and how much of each crude type to route into each gasoline product.
- The optimization goal is to maximize total profit, defined in words as total gasoline sales revenue minus total crude procurement cost.
- Required business rules:
  - Each gasoline product must satisfy its minimum content requirement for the distinguished crude type $$r^\star$$.
  - The total amount of each crude type used in all gasoline production must not exceed the quantity available from inventory plus purchases.
  - Purchases from each price block must respect the block capacity and the ordering structure of the tiered market price.
  - If any product demand limits $$D_g$$ are provided, production and sales of each product must not exceed those limits.
  - All decision quantities must be nonnegative.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$p_{r,k}$$: the quantity of crude type $$r$$ purchased from market price block $$k$$.
  - $$x_{r,g}$$: the quantity of crude type $$r$$ blended into gasoline product $$g$$.
  - $$y_g$$: the total quantity of gasoline product $$g$$ produced.
  - $$z$$: the achieved total profit.

Input Format
{
  "sets": {
    "raw_materials": "List of identifiers for crude inputs, representing $$\mathcal{R}$$.",
    "products": "List of identifiers for gasoline outputs, representing $$\mathcal{G}$$.",
    "purchasable_raw_materials": "Subset of $$\mathcal{R}$$ that can be bought from the market.",
    "purchase_blocks": "For each purchasable crude type $$r$$, an ordered list of price blocks indexed by $$k \\in \\mathcal{K}_r$$."
  },
  "planning_horizon": {
    "horizon": "Single-period or multi-period planning set $$\\mathcal{T}$$. If the instance is single-period, $$|\\mathcal{T}| = 1$$."
  },
  "parameters": {
    "initial_inventory": "Vector $$I_r$$ for each $$r \\in \\mathcal{R}$$.",
    "selling_price": "Vector $$s_g$$ for each $$g \\in \\mathcal{G}$$.",
    "minimum_share": "Vector $$\\alpha_g$$ giving the minimum fraction of the distinguished crude $$r^\\star$$ required in each product $$g \\in \\mathcal{G}$$.",
    "distinguished_crude": "Identifier $$r^\\star \\in \\mathcal{R}$$ subject to the minimum share rule.",
    "block_unit_cost": "For each purchasable crude type $$r$$ and block $$k$$, the unit cost $$c_{r,k}$$.",
    "block_capacity": "For each purchasable crude type $$r$$ and block $$k$$, the maximum purchasable amount $$U_{r,k}$$.",
    "purchase_cap": "Optional total market purchase limit $$\\bar P_r$$ for each purchasable crude type $$r$$.",
    "demand_upper_bound": "Optional sales or production limit $$D_g$$ for each product $$g \\in \\mathcal{G}$$."
  },
  "decision_scope": {
    "procurement": "Choose purchase quantities by crude type and price block.",
    "blending": "Choose how much of each crude type is allocated to each gasoline product.",
    "production": "Choose output quantities for each gasoline product."
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "purchase_plan": {
    "shape": "Nested mapping or array of $$p_{r,k}$$ values, indexed by purchasable crude type $$r$$ and block $$k$$.",
    "meaning": "Quantity purchased from each market price block."
  },
  "blending_plan": {
    "shape": "Matrix $$|\\mathcal{R}| \\times |\\mathcal{G}|$$ containing $$x_{r,g}$$.",
    "meaning": "Quantity of each crude type blended into each gasoline product."
  },
  "production_plan": {
    "shape": "Vector of length $$|\\mathcal{G}|$$ containing $$y_g$$.",
    "meaning": "Total production quantity of each gasoline product."
  },
  "objective_value": {
    "shape": "Scalar $$z$$.",
    "meaning": "Optimal total profit."
  }
}

Example(s)

